// @run
// @log 7
// @log 7
// THE VARIANT-TABLE HALF OF D733 — the widened field-set pass read out of `uVariants` instead
// of `sNames`, and the one place where it keeps FIRST MATCH rather than demanding a unique
// claimant.
//
// `type Circle = { r: i64 }` and `type Dot = { r: i64 }` are BOTH union arms here, so neither
// has an `sNames` row at all (`collectS` skips a union member) and the struct pass has nothing
// to answer with. The recorded element `{r: i32}` then has TWO widened claimants in the
// variant table — which is exactly the case a unique-claimant gate would decline.
//
// IT DOES NOT DECLINE, AND THE REASON IS A FUNCTION RATHER THAN A JUDGEMENT. `objVariantName`
// is FIRST MATCH over `uVariants` by field-NAME set, with map (19) and nested-struct (15)
// tightening and NO scalar-code tightening at all, and it is literally the function
// `letObjLitVariantIdx` / `collectLocals`' D51 arm call to decide which arm the BINDING is.
// A first-match widened scan therefore answers the row the value already carries; the
// widening is a strict subset of what `objVariantName` accepts, so the two can only agree or
// the scan declines. Requiring uniqueness here would refuse precisely the layout-twin programs
// the value resolved without difficulty.
//
// On master this is `emitProgram: union-arm array elements are not supported in this
// position` — the i32-list floor's arm rung, reached because `arrLitIsRef` had no name to key
// the ref list under. With `r: i32` on both arms it runs on master; the `i64` is the widening.
import { reverse } from "std:array"

type Circle = { r: i64 }
type Dot = { r: i64 }
type DotB = { db: i32 }
type DotU = Dot | DotB
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq

// 1. through a generic std call, which is how the corpus cell spells it.
function leg1() {
  const c = { r: 7 }
  const dd = reverse([c])[0]
  print(dd.r)
}
leg1()

// 2. `reverse` is SCENERY: the bare list literal is the same defect.
function leg2() {
  const c = { r: 7 }
  const xs = [c]
  print(xs[0].r)
}
leg2()
